2013-12-06 02:31:12	[pdx]fas	Has anyone here set up NAT successfully on Ubuntu?
2013-12-06 02:31:26	[pdx]fas	I've got the vpn connectiong going good and I set up an ad-hoc network
2013-12-06 02:31:40	[pdx]fas	but any machin I connect to it can't reach 10.14.x.2
2013-12-06 02:31:44	[pdx]fas	machine*
2013-12-06 02:32:50	Fish	Maybe you wanna set up routing records manually on those machines
2013-12-06 02:34:15	[pdx]fas	Kinda nooby at routing
2013-12-06 02:34:24	[pdx]fas	route -n ...?
2013-12-06 02:35:18	[RedCops]bbc	[pdx]fas: I guess your Ubuntu can ping everything correctly?
2013-12-06 02:35:42	-->	[USS]matou (matou@ictf2013-fco.9j9.bp482g.IP) has joined #ictf
2013-12-06 02:36:32	[pdx]fas	yes, it can
2013-12-06 02:36:48	[RedCops]bbc	okay, then on your machines connected to the LAN, I suggest the following
2013-12-06 02:36:57	[RedCops]bbc	disable any network manager
2013-12-06 02:37:23	[RedCops]bbc	ip address add local_add/local_mask dev local_interface
2013-12-06 02:37:55	[RedCops]bbc	local_add being an address in your local network that you have chosen 
2013-12-06 02:38:25	[RedCops]bbc	same for local_mask (for example, 192.168.1.0/24)
2013-12-06 02:44:38	[pdx]fas	I'm trying to get my mac to connect to this ad-hoc network
2013-12-06 02:45:57	[RedCops]bbc	what do you mean by ad-hoc?
2013-12-06 02:46:26	[RedCops]bbc	if you haven't setup any dhcp locally, go for a fix IP address with the GUI on your mac
